{#
  ============================================================================
  BLACKBOX EXPORTER - managed by Ansible role: monitoring
  Do not edit on the Pi; changes are overwritten on the next deploy.
  ============================================================================

  ONE MODULE PER SERVICE, which is unusual, and generated rather than written.

  The probe cannot use the service's own name: *.hub.frey does not resolve
  inside a container (the Pi's resolver points at whatever DNS the campground
  handed out, and dnsmasq only answers on the AP radio), and reaching Traefik
  on the host address is blocked by ufw's default-deny INPUT. So every probe
  goes to the Traefik container over the shared proxy network, and the service
  name is carried in the Host header and the TLS SNI instead - both of which
  blackbox can only set per module.

  Prometheus turns a bare service name into the right module; see the
  service_probe job in prometheus.yml.
#}
{#
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  WHAT COUNTS AS ALIVE, for every generated module below.

  Written once here rather than above each module: there is one of these per
  service, so a comment inside the loop would be repeated seventeen times and
  the file would be mostly prose.

  302 is a PASS, and that is the whole point. A service behind forward auth
  redirects an unauthenticated request to the login page, so a 302 proves
  Traefik routed it, TLS worked and Authelia answered. 401 and 403 are the
  same story for the *arr API paths, which demand an X-Api-Key this probe
  deliberately does not have.

  404 is NOT in the list, deliberately. That is what Traefik returns when it
  has dropped a router - a rule that failed to parse, or a middleware that
  does not exist yet - and it is the exact failure container metrics call
  healthy. Leaving 404 out is what makes this probe worth running.

  502 and 503 are likewise failures: the router is there, the container
  behind it is not.

  Redirects are not followed, because following one would need DNS for
  auth.hub.frey, which is the thing that does not work in here. The redirect
  itself is the signal.

  The certificate is verified against the Frey root rather than skipped.
  Skipping would also throw away probe_ssl_earliest_cert_expiry, which is the
  metric that catches frey_pki quietly failing to renew.
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#}
{% set m = monitoring %}
{% set valid_status = [200, 204, 301, 302, 303, 307, 308, 401, 403] %}
modules:

{% for host in monitoring_probe_hostnames %}
  # {{ host }}.{{ frey.service_domain }}
  frey_{{ host }}:
    prober: http
    timeout: 15s
    http:
      method: GET
      headers:
        Host: {{ host }}.{{ frey.service_domain }}
      tls_config:
        server_name: {{ host }}.{{ frey.service_domain }}
        ca_file: /frey-ca.crt
        insecure_skip_verify: false
      valid_status_codes: {{ valid_status }}
      valid_http_versions: ["HTTP/1.1", "HTTP/2.0"]
      follow_redirects: false
      preferred_ip_protocol: ip4
      ip_protocol_fallback: false
{% endfor %}
{% if monitoring_probe_extra_targets | default([]) | length > 0 %}
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # For monitoring_probe_extra_targets: things reached by address, where the
  # URL is the target and there is no Host header to fake.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  frey_url:
    prober: http
    timeout: 15s
    http:
      method: GET
      tls_config:
        ca_file: /frey-ca.crt
        insecure_skip_verify: false
      valid_status_codes: {{ valid_status }}
      follow_redirects: false
      preferred_ip_protocol: ip4
      ip_protocol_fallback: false
{% endif %}

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # Kept for hand-run checks from the Prometheus UI - probing a TCP port or a
  # host that answers no HTTP at all. Not scraped by any job.
  # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  frey_tcp:
    prober: tcp
    timeout: 10s
    tcp:
      preferred_ip_protocol: ip4

  # No icmp module on purpose. The prober needs CAP_NET_RAW, and this container
  # runs as an unprivileged uid with no-new-privileges, so an icmp module would
  # be listed, look available, and fail every time it was used. Pinging the
  # uplink gateway is done by the gateway host collector, which is root and can.

